Vendor Inventory Reconciliation service (Virs) 
	Low level Design (LLD) 
 
 
1. Purpose: 
 
This Low-level design doc describes the internal design of the vendor Inventory Reconciliation service including package structure, class responsibilities, database entities, Api flow, exception handling and validation strategy. 
 
2. Package structure: 

 
controller 
service 
repository 
entitity 
dto 
config 
exception 
aws 
util 
model 
 
3. Component Responsibilities 
 

Controller: Expose REST APIs and validate incoming requests. 

 

Service: Implement reconciliation business logic. 

 

Repository: Perform database operations using Spring Data JPA. 

 

AWS Adapter: Provide S3, SQS/SNS, and Secrets Manager abstraction. 

 

Validation: Validate CSV structure and business rules. 

 

Exception Handler: Handle and format application errors. 

 

4. Class Design 

 

ReconciliationController: REST endpoints for starting, querying, and re-triggering reconciliation. 

ReconciliationService: Coordinates validation, parsing, reconciliation, and persistence. 

CsvParserService: Parses CSV files into DTO objects. 

InventoryValidationService: Performs file and business validation. 

NotificationService: Publishes discrepancy notifications. 

InventoryRepository: Accesses internal inventory data. 

ReconciliationResultRepository: Stores reconciliation results. 

5. Entity Design 


InternalInventory: id, sku, quantity, warehouse, updatedAt 

VendorInventory: id, vendorId, sku, quantity, fileName, uploadedAt 

ReconciliationResult: id, batchId, sku, vendorQuantity, dbQuantity, status, remarks, createdAt 

BatchExecution: id, batchId, fileName, status, startedAt, completedAt 

 

6. DTO Design 

 

ReconciliationRequestDto: fileName 

ReconciliationResponseDto: batchId, status, processedRecords, mismatchCount 

ReconciliationResultDto: sku, vendorQuantity, dbQuantity, status, remarks 

 

7. REST API Design 

 

Method 

Endpoint 

Description 

 

POST 

/api/v1/reconciliation/start 

Start reconciliation for a file. 

 

GET 

/api/v1/reconciliation/{batchId} 

Get reconciliation results. 

 

POST 

/api/v1/reconciliation/retrigger/{batchId} 

Re-trigger a completed batch. 

 

8. Sequence Flow 

 

Client -> Controller -> Validation -> CSV Parser -> Reconciliation Service -> Repository -> Database -> Notification Service -> Response 

9. Validation Rules 

 

File must exist. 

File must be CSV. 

SKU cannot be empty. 

Quantity must be non-negative. 

Duplicate SKU records in the same file are not allowed. 

 

10. Exception Handling 

 

FileNotFoundException: Input file is missing. 

InvalidCsvFormatException: CSV structure is invalid. 

DuplicateRecordException: Duplicate SKU found. 

DatabaseConnectionException: Database is unavailable. 

NotificationPublishException: Notification could not be published. 

 

11. Logging Strategy 

 

Batch start and completion. 

File validation result. 

Record counts processed. 

Mismatch counts. 

Notification publication status. 

Error details with stack trace. 
 

12. Content Negotiation 
 

The query API supports application/json and text/csv responses. The response format is selected using the HTTP Accept header. 
 

13. API Versioning 
 

URI versioning is used for all APIs, for example /api/v1/reconciliation/start. This strategy is easy to understand and maintain. 
 

14. Testing Strategy 
 

Unit tests for service layer. 

Repository tests using an in-memory database. 

Controller tests using MockMvc. 

Positive and negative test cases. 

Edge cases such as empty files and duplicate records. 

15. AI Usage Documentation 
 
A separate document will be maintained to record which parts of the implementation were assisted by AI tools and which parts were implemented independently. 

 
 
 
 
 
 

 
 